Anticonvulsant-Induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) secondary to Carbamazepine therapy.

## 1. Case Scenario and Patient Narrative

### Initial Information
* Report Type: Initial Spontaneous Report
* Country of Origin: India
* Primary Source: Healthcare Professional (Dermatologist / Neurologist)

### Patient Demographics
* Patient Initials: S.K.
* Age: 34 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 72 kg
* Relevant Medical History: Focal epilepsy (newly diagnosed), no prior history of cutaneous drug reactions, no chronic co-morbidities.

### Suspected and Concomitant Drugs
* Suspected Drug: Carbamazepine 200 mg orally twice daily (Indication: Focal Epilepsy; Duration: Initiated 21 days prior to emergency admission).
* Interacting Drug: None.
* Concomitant Drug: Levetiracetam 500 mg orally twice daily (Initiated in ICU as safe replacement anticonvulsant therapy).

### Case Narrative and Timeline
* Day 1: Patient initiated on Carbamazepine 200 mg twice daily following diagnosis of focal seizures.
* Day 18: Patient developed high-grade fever (38.8°C), sore throat, and bilateral conjunctival injection.
* Day 21: Patient developed widespread, painful erythematous macules on face and trunk, accompanied by painful oral mucosal ulcers and lip crusting.
* Day 22: Epidermal detachment extended to 8 percent of total body surface area (TBSA). Presented to the Emergency Department in India.
* Admission Diagnostics:
  * Skin Biopsy: Full-thickness epidermal necrosis with subepidermal bullae formation, confirming Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS).
  * ALT / AST: 85 U/L / 78 U/L (Mild transaminase elevation).
  * Viral Screening (HSV, Mycoplasma, EBV): Negative.
* Action Taken: Carbamazepine was immediately and permanently discontinued. Patient admitted to Intensive Care Unit (ICU) for barrier isolation, continuous fluid resuscitation, systemic cyclosporine, and specialized ophthalmic management.
* Outcome: On Day 14 post-discontinuation, skin lesions fully re-epithelialized and mucosal ulcerations healed, leaving minor conjunctival scarring.

